# Topic: cold charges booked where they belong
#
# What it does: each interpreted instruction starts a frame with an armed opcode marker. Its first dispatch charges its own cold
# code and restores its normal opcode; later dispatches take the normal path. Only instructions actually dispatched are rearmed
# at the frame boundary. Coverage belongs to the prototype, so simultaneous activations, re-entry and suspended coroutines share
# the same once-per-frame charge without an execution cursor. Code skipped by a jump or left after an error is never charged.
# Auxiliary words and closure captures consumed as data are not dispatches. Tracking records live outside the VM heap, and a
# collected prototype is forgotten before its code is freed. A profile books each code charge to the executing frame and pc.
# Armed opcodes use the normal switch's fallback. Listing them as cases made MSVC compress its hot dispatch through an extra
# lookup table; keeping the normal opcode range avoids that cost and adds no warm instruction check or call hook.
# A profile also keeps kinds of work apart in each instruction's and
# node's booking (lua_profilesplit: cold, the collector's, dispatch; Studio-era and defaulted prices), books a function's cold code
# to its own instructions and a callee's first-touch slots to the callee, keeps each cold charge for an object (a table by the
# instruction that made it, a C function, a host function) with who paid it and whether anything else touched it in the frame
# (lua_profilecoldobjects; the exclusive share per instruction and node), books what the host charges next to a thread about to
# resume or a handler about to run (lua_profilebooknext), tells the host which instruction a booking goes to now
# (lua_profilecurrent) and how often a thread was resumed (lua_profileresumes: two readings a yield came between), keeps each function's lines per phase, and books the work of the binding's helper chunks ("=__mlua...")
# to the script instruction that called them, as the host's own Luau's is.
#
# Why upstream lacks it: a host's work clock models a client's cold caches; a real CPU pays them itself, and Roblox's profilers
# live in its engine.

diff --git a/luau/Common/include/Luau/Bytecode.h b/luau/Common/include/Luau/Bytecode.h
index 0574fbe..ef19b95 100644
--- a/luau/Common/include/Luau/Bytecode.h
+++ b/luau/Common/include/Luau/Bytecode.h
@@ -511,7 +511,11 @@ enum LuauOpcode
 
 // Bytecode instruction header: it's always a 32-bit integer, with low byte (first byte in little endian) containing the opcode
 // Some instruction types require more data and have more 32-bit integers following the header
-#define LUAU_INSN_OP(insn) ((insn) & 0xff)
+// The interpreter disarms a cold instruction's marker before executing it. Semantic readers
+// still see its opcode, including lookahead for fast calls and the debugger's bytecode walks.
+#define LUAU_INSN_COLD 0x80
+static_assert(LOP__COUNT <= LUAU_INSN_COLD, "the cold marker needs an unused opcode bit");
+#define LUAU_INSN_OP(insn) ((insn) & 0x7f)
 
 // ABC encoding: three 8-bit values, containing registers or small numbers
 #define LUAU_INSN_A(insn) (((insn) >> 8) & 0xff)
